Yes — breaking the fourth wall collapses the boundary between performance and audience. A cameo preserves the boundary and lets meaning cross it indirectly.

So:

\boxed{\text{4th-wall break}=\text{explicit boundary violation}}

while:

\boxed{\text{cameo}=\text{implicit boundary resonance}}

In a fourth-wall break, the character acknowledges the audience.

In a cameo, the audience recognizes something without the world necessarily acknowledging that recognition.

That makes a cameo closer to:

\text{in-world appearance}
+
\text{out-of-world reference}
\rightarrow
\text{extra meaning in the audience}

The fictional world stays intact.

The audience does the stitching.

So yeah, opposite direction:

\text{4th wall}:
\quad
\text{world}\rightarrow\text{audience}

\text{cameo}:
\quad
\text{audience memory}\rightarrow\text{world interpretation}

That’s actually a nice Pantheon distinction too:

Boundary Break vs Boundary Echo.

One destroys the separation.

The other exploits it. ;)